          "text": "The dominant pattern is elemental Sr in a **triclinically strained fcc lattice**. The fourfold splitting near 25.3\u00b0 and threefold splitting near 29.4\u00b0 correspond to the split cubic \\(\\{111\\}\\) and \\(\\{200\\}\\) families. Higher-angle multiplets are consistent with the same metric.\n\nAn equivalent pseudo-conventional F-centered cell is approximately:\n\n- \\(a=6.098\\), \\(b=6.074\\), \\(c=6.020\\) \u00c5  \n- \\(\\alpha=89.99^\\circ\\), \\(\\beta=89.70^\\circ\\), \\(\\gamma=89.42^\\circ\\)\n\nThe CIF below uses its primitive triclinic cell.\n\n```cif\ndata_Sr_triclinically_strained_fcc\n_audit_creation_method              'Indexed from supplied Cu Kalpha1 powder pattern'\n_chemical_name_common               'Strontium, triclinically strained fcc'\n_chemical_formula_structural        'Sr'\n_chemical_formula_sum               'Sr'\n_cell_formula_units_Z               1\n\n_cell_length_a                       4.2763\n_cell_length_b                       4.2960\n_cell_length_c                       4.3250\n_cell_angle_alpha                    59.462\n_cell_angle_beta                     59.577\n_cell_angle_gamma                    59.949\n_cell_volume                         55.74\n\n_space_group_name_H-M_alt            'P -1'\n_space_group_IT_number               2\n\n_diffrn_radiation_type               'Cu Kalpha1'\n_diffrn_radiation_wavelength         1.5406\n\nloop_\n_space_group_symop_id\n_space_group_symop_operation_xyz\n1  'x,y,z'\n2  '-x,-y,-z'\n\nloop_\n_atom_type_symbol\n_atom_type_oxidation_number\nSr  0\n\nloop_\n_atom_site_label\n_atom_site_type_symbol\n_atom_site_symmetry_multiplicity\n_atom_site_Wyckoff_symbol\n_atom_site_fract_x\n_atom_site_fract_y\n_atom_site_fract_z\n_atom_site_occupancy\nSr1  Sr  1  a  0.000000  0.000000  0.000000  1.000\n```\n\nThe average undistorted parent would be cubic \\(Fm\\bar 3m\\) Sr with \\(a\\approx6.064\\) \u00c5, but that model does not reproduce the clearly resolved peak splitting. Weak features below about 1% of the principal intensity may represent background, minute superstructure scattering, or a trace secondary component; they do not warrant a separate bulk structural model.",
